1. `option_bag_junk_drawer` — `describeCheckout`

The function reads six unrelated-looking properties from one broad bag. An agent adding a checkout attribute may append another field without knowing whether it belongs to identity, location, or execution state.

Refactor into cohesive, explicitly named records and destructure at the boundary:

```ts
interface CheckoutContext {
  plan: string;
  role: string;
}

interface CheckoutLocale {
  region: string;
  currency: string;
}

interface CheckoutState {
  status: string;
  shouldRetry: boolean;
}

export function describeCheckout(
  context: CheckoutContext,
  locale: CheckoutLocale,
  state: CheckoutState,
): string {
  const { plan, role } = context;
  const { region, currency } = locale;
  const { status, shouldRetry } = state;

  return [
    plan,
    role,
    region,
    currency,
    status,
    String(shouldRetry),
  ].join(":");
}
```

This ties each new field to an owned concept instead of turning `CheckoutOptions` into an ever-growing junk drawer.

2. `return_shape_roulette` — `resolvePaymentResult`

The function returns three shapes with zero overlap between some variants and no explicit return type. An agent could access `result.ok`, `result.url`, or `result.value` as though it always existed, or add a fourth ambiguous shape.

Use a named discriminated union:

```ts
type PaymentResult =
  | {
      kind: "error";
      code: string | undefined;
      message: string | undefined;
    }
  | {
      kind: "redirect";
      url: string | undefined;
      permanent: false;
    }
  | {
      kind: "success";
      value: string | undefined;
      cached: false;
    };

export function resolvePaymentResult(
  input: PaymentResultInput,
): PaymentResult {
  if (input.error) {
    return {
      kind: "error",
      code: input.code,
      message: input.message,
    };
  }

  if (input.redirect) {
    return {
      kind: "redirect",
      url: input.url,
      permanent: false,
    };
  }

  return {
    kind: "success",
    value: input.value,
    cached: false,
  };
}
```

Callers must now narrow on `kind`, while the explicit return type prevents future branches from silently inventing a new contract. A follow-up could also discriminate the input so `error` and `redirect` cannot both be true.

3. `negative_flag_maze` — `canRunBilling`

`!disableBilling && !skipRetry` requires two mental inversions. An agent extending it could apply De Morgan’s law incorrectly, negate only one flag, or misunderstand whether `skipRetry` permits billing.

Expose positive states and return the predicate directly:

```ts
interface BillingRunPolicy {
  isBillingEnabled: boolean;
  isRetryAllowed: boolean;
}

export function canRunBilling({
  isBillingEnabled,
  isRetryAllowed,
}: BillingRunPolicy): boolean {
  return isBillingEnabled && isRetryAllowed;
}
```

This also resolves the related `boolean_naming_drift`: both names read as booleans and the condition states the permitted case directly.

Before changing callers, add a four-case truth-table test for `canRunBilling`, one test for the exact checkout string order, and one test per `PaymentResult.kind`.
